// @run
// @log 1
// @log 1.5
// @log -2.25
// @log 0.25
// `f64.load` — the 64-bit twin of `load-f32.vl`. Each double is laid down as two
// i32 stores, LOW word first (little-endian), so the high word carries the sign,
// exponent and top mantissa bits:
//
//   0x3FF0000000000000 =  1.0     0x3FF8000000000000 =  1.5
//   0xC002000000000000 = -2.25    0x3FD0000000000000 =  0.25
//
// All four are exact binary values, so the decimal render is exact.
//
// Note what this case proves beyond the instruction: the program spells `f64`
// NOWHERE — no annotation, no float literal — yet `print` routes to the
// `__print_f64__` import. The emitter's import scan reads the intrinsic's result
// width off the NAME (`repMemLoadScalarMask`), because a missed import is
// invalid wasm rather than a wrong number.
__store_i32__(0, 0x00000000)
__store_i32__(4, 0x3FF00000)
print(__load_f64__(0))
__store_i32__(8, 0x00000000)
__store_i32__(12, 0x3FF80000)
print(__load_f64__(8))
// 0xC0020000 has its top bit set, so the hex spelling would be an i64 (a VL
// integer literal takes the narrowest type holding it exactly) and
// `__store_i32__` would reject it — written as the signed i32 with those bits.
__store_i32__(16, 0x00000000)
__store_i32__(20, -1073610752)
print(__load_f64__(16))
__store_i32__(24, 0x00000000)
__store_i32__(28, 0x3FD00000)
print(__load_f64__(24))
